  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: A cold front pushing into the region late \nSaturday will bring additional chances for scattered showers and\nthunderstorms. \n\nA more potent upper level shortwave ejecting south out of Canada\ntomorrow will bring with it a much stronger cold front into the\nregion late Saturday night into Sunday morning. Ahead of this\ncold front, plenty of warm and moist air will allow widely\nscattered showers and thunderstorms to develop across the Ohio\nvalley and translate east through the late afternoon and evening\nhours. Current CAM guidance keeps the timing of these storms\nmoving into our region during the late evening hours and early\novernight hours. While this typically does not bode well with\nthunderstorm strength and activity, the amount of forcing for\nascent from the cold front and the aforementioned upper level\nshortwave may overcome the lack or waning instability to keep \nthunderstorm activity going longer than is typical.\nNevertheless, the 0-6 Km deep layer shear looks to increase to\naround 30-40 knots for the area based. This wind shear is\nsupported with HREF probabilities above 50% in some cases for\nshear values above 30 knots. Some lingering instability of\n500-1000 J/Kg of SBCAPE will help support storm development as\nthe front approaches. As the front pushes through the area\nduring the late evening and early overnight hours, some\nlingering showers may still be ongoing across the Piedmont of VA\nand NC during the early morning hours before clearing builds\nacross the area on Sunday with the injection of drier air from\nthe northwest. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: After continued showers and thunderstorms on \nSaturday, drier weather returns Sunday and continues into early \nnext week.\n\nBehind the aforementioned frontal passage, a much cooler and \ndrier airmass will settle into the region as high pressure build\neast across the Mid- Atlantic and southeast. Coolest air \narrives on Sunday and Monday with highs in the 70s in the \nmountains and low to mid 80s east of the mountains. Lows Sunday \nand Monday morning will drop into the 50s with a few locations \npossibly into the low 50s in the mountains. Expect foggy\nmornings across the mountains, especially in the river valleys,\nand cool dry afternoons for much of the region through Tuesday.\nSurface high pressure and upper level troughing across the\neastern Conus breaks down by Wednesday, which will allow showers\nand thunderstorms to occur across the area once again.",
